// Resolvers The resolvers in front of this server whose word on a client is
// believed, as addresses or CIDR prefixes. A query from one of them that
// carries the client subnet option of RFC 7871 is shown in the query
// stream with the client it names; one from anywhere else is shown by its
// own address, because the option is whatever its sender wrote. An empty
// list believes nobody, which is where a server starts. A bare address is
// stored as the single-host prefix it means. Nothing about an answer
// depends on this (docs/decisions/ D50).
type Resolvers = []string

// ReverseConflictPolicy What happens when an address already answers a reverse lookup with some
// other name (docs/decisions/ D3).
//
//   - `first-wins` keeps the entry that is there and reports the conflict.
//     The default, and the only value that never changes an answer nobody
//     asked to change.
//   - `last-wins` replaces a generated entry with the new one, still
//     reporting the conflict. An entry somebody wrote by hand is never
//     replaced.
//   - `multi` keeps both, which is the literal reading of "generate the
//     reverse entry" and turns a routine change into a multi-record PTR set
//     that reverse-lookup checks are not built for.
//   - `reject` refuses the whole change.
type ReverseConflictPolicy string

